  "text": "The recipe collection now has an **All / Full recipes / Recipe ideas** filter. It starts on All, and it works together with the existing title/tag search. `make test` passes (229 tests), `make typecheck` passes, and the 6 new JavaScript tests pass when run directly in Node. I haven't tried it in a real browser because starting a server was out of scope.\n\n**What changed**\n- **`app/templates/index.html`:** three radio buttons labelled \"Show\" sit under the search box, with All selected. They stay visible and usable even when nothing matches. The count line now announces changes to screen readers.\n- **`app/templates/partials/recipe_list.html`:** each card is marked as a full recipe or an idea, so the filter can tell them apart.\n- **`app/static/js/recipe_search.js`:** a card is shown only if it matches both the chosen type and the search words. Switching filters doesn't touch the search text, and the order of cards is unchanged.\n  - **Count:** with no search and All selected, the original text appears (\"N recipes in your collection\"). Otherwise it reads, for example, \"2 full recipes in your collection\", \"1 matching recipe idea\", or the existing \"N matching recipes\".\n  - **No matches:** the message explains why, e.g. `No recipe ideas match \"tart\". Try a different search or filter.` or \"No recipe ideas in your collection yet.\"\n  - **Back button:** the script now also runs when the page loads, so a search or filter the browser restores is applied straight away. Before, a restored search showed every recipe until you typed again.\n- **`app/static/css/app.css`:** a few rules to lay the radio buttons out in a row.\n\nNo new dependencies, and nothing else was changed.\n\n**Tests**\n- **`tests/js/recipe_search.test.mjs`:** runs the real script against a small fake page using Node's built-in test runner. It covers the default view and order, each filter, filter plus search with the search text kept, both no-match messages, and a restored search/filter on load.\n- **`tests/test_recipe_search_js.py`:** runs those JavaScript tests as part of `make test`, so CI picks them up. It skips if Node isn't installed.\n- **`tests/test_routes.py`:** a new test checks that the filter appears with All selected, that cards are marked by type, and that the order and count are unchanged.\n\nRunning `node --test tests/js/` on the whole folder fails on Node 26, so the test file is passed by name instead. The comment in the test file shows the command that works."
